msgid "metric_subtype_description_interval"
msgstr "The interval scale uses values that are arbitrary but quantifiable. The zero point on the scale is arbitrary, so negative values can be used. Examples of interval values are the year date in many calendars or temperature in the Celsius scale."

msgid "metric_subtype_description_ratioabsolute"
msgstr "The ratio absolute scale also has a natural zero point, but uses natural numbers. It would be used for example to measure the number of children in a family. It is also used for per cent measurements, e.g. probabilities measured in per cent."

msgid "metric_subtype_description_rationonabsolute"
msgstr "Like the interval scale, the ratio non-absolute scale also allows the comparison of values which are in essence arbitrary, but has a natural zero point. Most physical quantities, such as length in centimetres or duration in seconds are measured on ratio scales. The natural zero point allows for the ratio between values to be calculated, even when different measurement scales are being used (for example km/h and m/h)."

